---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { SITE } from "../../lib/site";
import { localizePath , pick } from "../../i18n/utils";

const lang = (Astro.currentLocale ?? "en") as "en" | "zh";

const slug = "sftp-file-browser";

const C = pick({
  en: {
    heading: "Using the SFTP file browser",
    intro:
      "VVTerm includes a built-in SFTP browser so you can move, preview, and edit remote files without a separate app. Here is how to use it.",
    meta: "Documentation",
    title: "Using the SFTP file browser — VVTerm",
    description:
      "Browse remote folders, upload and download files, create folders, rename, move, delete, preview text, images, and video, and edit POSIX permissions in VVTerm.",
    schemaDescription:
      "Use the VVTerm SFTP file browser to browse remote folders, upload and download, create directories, rename, move, delete, preview, and edit files and permissions.",
    bodyHtml: `
  <p>
    Every server you connect to over SSH can also be browsed over SFTP in the same app. The file
    browser runs on top of your existing connection, so there is nothing extra to configure on the
    server — if you can SSH in, you can manage files.
  </p>

  <h2>Opening the file browser</h2>
  <p>
    From a connected server, open the <strong>Files</strong> view to start browsing. VVTerm lists the
    remote directory contents, showing names, sizes, and types. On Mac the browser sits beside your
    terminal in the same window; on iPhone and iPad it opens as its own view so you can switch between
    the shell and your files.
  </p>

  <h2>Browsing folders</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Tap or click a folder to open it, and use the path or back navigation to move up.</li>
    <li>Start from your home directory and walk into any path your account can read.</li>
    <li>Items you do not have permission to enter are limited by the server, just as they would be in a shell.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Uploading and downloading</h2>
  <p>
    Move files in both directions directly from the browser:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Download</strong> a remote file to your device to keep a local copy.</li>
    <li><strong>Upload</strong> a file from your device to the current remote folder.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This is the quickest way to pull down a log, push up a config, or move assets between machines
    without dropping to a command line.
  </p>

  <h2>Managing files and folders</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Create folders</strong> to organize remote directories.</li>
    <li><strong>Rename</strong> files and folders in place.</li>
    <li><strong>Move</strong> items to a different path.</li>
    <li><strong>Delete</strong> files and folders you no longer need.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    These actions run over SFTP against the live server, so changes take effect immediately.
  </p>

  <h2>Previewing files</h2>
  <p>
    You can preview common file types without downloading them first. VVTerm renders <strong>text</strong>, <strong>images</strong>, and <strong>video</strong> inline, which makes it
    easy to check a file's contents — confirm a config, glance at a screenshot, or scrub a recording —
    before deciding what to do with it.
  </p>

  <h2>Editing text files</h2>
  <p>
    For text files, the browser includes an editor so you can make a quick change to a config or
    script and save it straight back to the server. There is no need to copy a file down, edit it
    locally, and upload it again for small edits.
  </p>

  <h2>Editing permissions</h2>
  <p>
    On servers that support it, you can view and change a file's <strong>POSIX permissions</strong> —
    the read, write, and execute bits for owner, group, and others. This is handy for making a script
    executable or tightening access on a sensitive file without typing <code>chmod</code> by hand.
  </p>

  <h2>Next steps</h2>
  <p>
    File access depends on the account you log in as, set in <a href="/docs/adding-a-server">Adding a server</a> and <a href="/docs/authentication-and-keys">Authentication and SSH keys</a>. To keep your servers
    consistent across devices, see <a href="/docs/icloud-sync">iCloud sync and Keychain</a>.
  </p>
`,
  },
  zh: {
    heading: "使用 SFTP 文件浏览器",
    intro:
      "VVTerm 内置了一个 SFTP 浏览器，让你无需另装应用即可移动、预览和编辑远程文件。这里说明如何使用它。",
    meta: "文档",
    title: "使用 SFTP 文件浏览器 — VVTerm",
    description:
      "在 VVTerm 中浏览远程文件夹、上传和下载文件、新建文件夹、重命名、移动、删除、预览文本、图片和视频，并编辑 POSIX 权限。",
    schemaDescription:
      "使用 VVTerm 的 SFTP 文件浏览器浏览远程文件夹、上传和下载、新建目录、重命名、移动、删除、预览，以及编辑文件和权限。",
    bodyHtml: `
  <p>
    你通过 SSH 连接的每台服务器，都能在同一个应用里通过 SFTP 浏览。文件浏览器运行在你现有的连接之上，因此服务器端无需额外配置 —— 只要你能 SSH 登录，就能管理文件。
  </p>

  <h2>打开文件浏览器</h2>
  <p>
    在已连接的服务器中，打开 <strong>Files</strong> 视图开始浏览。VVTerm 会列出远程目录的内容，显示名称、大小和类型。在 Mac 上，浏览器与终端并排在同一个窗口里；在 iPhone 和 iPad 上，它作为独立视图打开，方便你在 shell 和文件之间切换。
  </p>

  <h2>浏览文件夹</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>点按或单击一个文件夹将其打开，并用路径或返回导航向上移动。</li>
    <li>从你的主目录开始，进入你的账户能读取的任意路径。</li>
    <li>你没有权限进入的项目会受服务器限制，就像在 shell 中一样。</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>上传与下载</h2>
  <p>
    直接在浏览器中双向移动文件：
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>下载</strong>一个远程文件到你的设备，保留本地副本。</li>
    <li><strong>上传</strong>一个文件从你的设备到当前远程文件夹。</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    这是拉取日志、推送配置或在机器间移动资源的最快方式，无需切换到命令行。
  </p>

  <h2>管理文件和文件夹</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>新建文件夹</strong>来整理远程目录。</li>
    <li>就地<strong>重命名</strong>文件和文件夹。</li>
    <li>把项目<strong>移动</strong>到不同路径。</li>
    <li><strong>删除</strong>你不再需要的文件和文件夹。</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    这些操作通过 SFTP 直接对实时服务器执行，因此更改会立即生效。
  </p>

  <h2>预览文件</h2>
  <p>
    你可以预览常见文件类型而无需先下载。VVTerm 会内联渲染<strong>文本</strong>、<strong>图片</strong>和<strong>视频</strong>，让你在决定如何处理之前轻松查看文件内容 —— 确认一份配置、瞥一眼截图，或拖动浏览一段录像。
  </p>

  <h2>编辑文本文件</h2>
  <p>
    对于文本文件，浏览器内置了一个编辑器，让你能快速改动配置或脚本并直接保存回服务器。小幅修改无需把文件下载下来、本地编辑再上传回去。
  </p>

  <h2>编辑权限</h2>
  <p>
    在支持的服务器上，你可以查看并更改文件的 <strong>POSIX 权限</strong> —— 即所有者、组和其他人的读、写、执行位。这对于把脚本设为可执行，或在不手动输入 <code>chmod</code> 的情况下收紧敏感文件的访问很方便。
  </p>

  <h2>后续步骤</h2>
  <p>
    文件访问取决于你登录所用的账户，它在<a href="/zh/docs/adding-a-server">添加服务器</a>和<a href="/zh/docs/authentication-and-keys">认证与 SSH 密钥</a>中设置。要让你的服务器在设备间保持一致，参见 <a href="/zh/docs/icloud-sync">iCloud 同步与 Keychain</a>。
  </p>
`,
  },
}, lang);

const articleSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "TechArticle",
  name: C.heading,
  description: C.schemaDescription,
  url: `${SITE.siteUrl}${localizePath(`/docs/${slug}`, lang)}`,
};
---

<DocsLayout
  kind="docs"
  activeSlug={slug}
  heading={C.heading}
  intro={C.intro}
  meta={C.meta}
  title={C.title}
  description={C.description}
  canonicalPath={`/docs/${slug}`}
  structuredData={[articleSchema]}
>
  <Fragment set:html={C.bodyHtml} />
</DocsLayout>
